            <ArticleTitle>Analgesic properties of enkephalinase inhibitors: animal and human studies.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>363-70</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>D-phenylalanine, bacitracin and puromycin produce long-lasting, naloxone-reversible analgesia in mice. Analgesic potency parallels potency of these compounds as inhibitors of met-enkephalin degradation by mouse brain enzymes. D-phenylalanine potentiates acupuncture analgesia in mice and humans and has been used to ameliorate a variety of human chronic pain conditions.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
